  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nConditions will be warm and mostly dry to kick off the work week \nthanks to a ridge of high pressure present over the region. First \nthough, temperatures will dip down into the 30s and 40s tonight with \nareas more west staying a bit more moderate due to increasing cloud \ncover. After the cool start Monday morning, temperatures will climb \ninto the 60s and low 70s. Temperatures will trend warmer through \nmidweek with warm days and mild nights. \n\nWhile conditions will be rain-free for most during the first half of \nthe week, showers cannot be ruled out on Tuesday. A surface low \npressure system will develop over the Midwest late Monday. Extending \nfrom the low will be a warm front that partially lifts through the \nregion. Depending on the placement of the front, some passing \nshowers will be possible during the day on Tuesday, mainly across \nnorthern portions of Central NY. With enough moisture, most models \nare showing some showers though the short range guidance are coming \nin with wider coverage. Given this, slight chance PoPs were added to \nareas north of the Southern Tier for now. Model trends will need to \nbe monitored over the next few forecast cycles.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe aforementioned surface low will push a cold front through the \nregion late in the week. Showers will move in on Thursday and \ncontinue into Friday. The front may not fully depart the region as a \nanother low pressure system forms over the Atlantic. As this low \nmoves through, moisture will get advected inland and showers will \nlinger into the Weekend. A dry slot will also move into the region \nand this could help keep some locations dry while others have a \nrainy start to the weekend.\n\nFollowing the frontal system, temperatures will trend cooler into \nthe weekend. Even so, temperatures will generally remain above \naverage for this time of year.",
